{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Daniel Mercer woke up with a jolt, his fingers twitching against the cool sheets as his eyes flickered open. Sunlight spilled through the curtains, but something felt off—his room was too perfect, the angles unnaturally sharp, like a render glitch. He sat up and flexed his hand; when he pressed his thumb into his palm, a faint *ding* sound echoed in his head. His coffee table displayed a health bar floating above his morning cereal box, numbers ticking down as he ate. Panic clawed at his throat when he glanced at his phone; the screen was frozen on a loading icon, pixels shimmering like static. Then his cat, Whiskers, turned its head slowly, pupils dilating into perfect black circles before it spoke in a distorted voice: *\"Welcome to Beta Test, Player One.\"*\n\nBy noon, Daniel had confirmed his worst fear: he was trapped in *Eclipse Horizon*, a death-march RPG that had gone gold months ago. NPCs repeated dialogue loops, and his character sheet now overlay his vision, stats like *Sanity* and *NPC Trust* dropping with every interaction. He tried to \"debuff\" the system by smashing his head against the wall—no damage. Instead, a quest marker materialized: *\"Find the Glitch in the Code (Optional).\"* Whiskers, now perched on his shoulder like a cyberpunk familiar, mewed, *\"Level up your paranoia, boss.\"* As Daniel stared at his reflection in the mirror, his own eyes flickered for a split second with a loading bar. He whispered, *\"I need to find a save file.\"* The mirror’s surface rippled, and a debug console materialized across his vision, blinking: *\"ERROR: REALITY CORRUPTED. INSERT DISC TO CONTINUE.\"*", "logprobs": [{"token": "Daniel", "logprob": -1.6121129989624023}, {"token": " Mercer", "logprob": -0.27265679836273193}, {"token": " woke", "logprob": -1.2407020330429077}, {"token": " up", "logprob": -0.637060284614563}, {"token": "